ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-013--sha-256-and-chacha20/design-doc/01-sha-256-and-chacha20-corpus-project.md:36:Create one project, `corpus/projects/sha-chacha/`, with two independently callable kernels. SHA-256 hashes a short ASCII message using one padded block and the full 64-round schedule. ChaCha20 produces one 64-byte keystream block using all 20 rounds. Both run on RV32IMF with integer instructions. RV32IMF lacks rotate instructions, so the shift/OR forms expose bit-vector rewrite opportunities whose value must be judged with the available instructions. SHA-256 supplies indexed round constants and loop-carried copies. ChaCha20 keeps 16 working words live across each double round, giving the allocator a workload where spills, call boundaries, and coalescing can affect runtime.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-013--sha-256-and-chacha20/design-doc/01-sha-256-and-chacha20-corpus-project.md:89:Write one loop of ten double rounds. Each iteration calls `quarter` on columns `(0,4,8,12) (1,5,9,13) (2,6,10,14) (3,7,11,15)`, then diagonals `(0,5,10,15) (1,6,11,12) (2,7,8,13) (3,4,9,14)`. Immediately copy the four returned fields to their matching locals before the next call. Eight explicit calls follow the fixed schedule without needing a permutation table or indexed state. After the loop, add each initial word back to the matching working word and write the 16 sums to `out`.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-013--sha-256-and-chacha20/design-doc/01-sha-256-and-chacha20-corpus-project.md:108:The browser already reads `ingest` in `src/workbench/projects.ts`. Add optional `ingest?: IngestOptions` to `ProjectMeta` and forward it from both `Program.fromProject` and `ingestProject`, preserving their defaults. This lets the existing browser and Node project paths read the same file. In `tools/reference-check.mjs`, pass the reference compiler's `r.signatures` as the fourth argument to `imageFromCode` so direct calls to `quarter` read all four return words. Pass signatures in the corpus-wide reference tests in `tests/typed/backend.test.mjs` as well.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-013--sha-256-and-chacha20/design-doc/01-sha-256-and-chacha20-corpus-project.md:110:A small Node probe using `tools/node-vendor.mjs` confirmed that the default project loader failed on `new Int32Array(64)` with `unknown tape opcode 252 at 9`. Opening the same source through the workbench with tape metadata succeeded. Direct tape ingest, the evaluator, the reference image, and the checked `speed` image agreed on wrapping addition, bitwise operations, a four-word value result, and all five written u16 cells. The standalone reference helper returned all four words with signatures supplied and a single scalar with the current runner arguments. A separate 55-cell probe confirmed zero initialization, dynamic indexing, positive integer division, and variable shifts for word packing in the evaluator and reference image.
ttmp/2026/11/26/IRWB-013--sha-256-and-chacha20/design-doc/01-sha-256-and-chacha20-corpus-project.md:120:Report the emulator's executed instructions divided by **64** for each entry and revision. SHA always processes one 64-byte padded compression block, even for the empty message. ChaCha always emits 64 bytes. Include the kernel's setup and stores in the count. In the fresh script, compare r0 and `speed` on a fixed 55-character ASCII message and the RFC section 2.3.2 ChaCha case, keeping backend options the same. Use the `coalesce`, `chains`, and `homes` options and inspect the hot loop listings for copies, shifts, and spills. A hand-tuned ceiling can live in a separate project if a baseline variant is needed.
